\section{Confounding in LLM Evaluation}
\label{sec:confounding}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{fig2_ovb_bias}
\caption{Sequential estimation bias ($\hat{\theta}_{\mathrm{seq}} - \theta_{\mathrm{true}}$) versus true contamination susceptibility $c_n$. Pearson $r = 0.86$ ($p < 0.001$) confirms systematic overestimation of contaminated models. Synthetic data ($N = 50$, $I = 100$, $J = 8$, 20 seeds).}
\label{fig:ovb_bias}
\end{figure}

The Many-Facet Rasch Model (MFRM) extends classical Rasch measurement to settings with multiple rater facets \citep{linacre1989mfrm, linacre1994mfrm}. Originating in educational measurement, the model treats rater variation as a nuisance facet to be estimated and removed rather than a source of noise to be averaged over. In the LLM evaluation context, where $J$ judges score $N$ models across $I$ items, the MFRM specifies
%
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:mfrm}
\log \frac{P(Y_{nij} \geq k \mid \theta_n, b_i, d_j, \tau_k)}{P(Y_{nij} < k \mid \theta_n, b_i, d_j, \tau_k)} = \theta_n - b_i - d_j - \tau_k,
\end{equation}
%
where $Y_{nij}$ is the ordinal rating by judge $j$ for model $n$ on item $i$, $\theta_n$ is the ability of model $n$ ($n = 1, \ldots, N$), $b_i$ is the difficulty of item $i$ ($i = 1, \ldots, I$), $d_j$ is the severity of judge $j$ ($j = 1, \ldots, J$), and $\tau_k$ is the threshold for category $k$. All facets reside on a common logit scale, so model ability is directly comparable after adjustment for item difficulty and judge severity. \citet{jiao2025mfrm_llm} applied this framework to compare ten LLM raters with human experts, showing that MFRM separates genuine ability from systematic judge effects in multi-judge panels.

However, standard MFRM assumes that all systematic sources of score variation are captured by the modeled facets. This assumption breaks down when some models have been exposed to benchmark items during training \citep{dcr2025}. Such contamination inflates scores on exposed items through memorization rather than genuine ability. We represent this effect as an interaction $c_n \cdot e_i$, where $c_n \geq 0$ is the contamination susceptibility of model $n$ and $e_i \in [0, 1]$ is the exposure level of item $i$. The complete data-generating process becomes
%
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:full}
\eta_{nijk} = \theta_n - b_i - d_j - \tau_k + c_n \cdot e_i.
\end{equation}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{fig4_bias_vs_rho}
\caption{Sequential estimator bias remains approximately constant across confounding levels ($\rho$), confirming omission-driven rather than confounding-strength-dependent bias. Joint G-MFRM estimation reduces bias by approximately 70\% at all $\rho$ levels. Synthetic data: $N = 50$, $I = 100$, $J = 8$, 50 seeds.}
\label{fig:bias_vs_rho}
\end{figure}

When a practitioner fits the standard MFRM (Eq.~\ref{eq:mfrm}) to data generated by Eq.~\ref{eq:full}, the contamination term is absent from the estimated model. Its influence does not vanish but is absorbed into the estimated parameters $\tilde{\theta}_n$, $\tilde{b}_i$, and $\tilde{d}_j$, which distorts all downstream inferences.

The consequence of this omission can be stated precisely.

\begin{observation}[Omitted Variable Bias in MFRM]
\label{obs:ovb}
Let the full model be $\eta_{nijk} = \theta_n - b_i - d_j - \tau_k + c_n \cdot e_i$ and the short model omit the contamination term: $\tilde{\eta}_{nijk} = \tilde{\theta}_n - \tilde{b}_i - \tilde{d}_j - \tau_k$. Under a linear approximation to the ordinal logit link \citep{wooldridge2010, angrist2009mostly},\footnote{The OVB formula is exact for linear models. We apply it to the MFRM linear predictor $\eta$, which is an approximation. Synthetic validation confirms the approximation quality: the Pearson correlation between the predicted bias direction and empirical bias reaches $r = 0.86$ ($p < 0.001$), indicating that the linear result accurately characterizes bias direction and relative magnitude.}
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:ovb}
\mathrm{plim}(\tilde{\theta}_n - \theta_n) = \gamma_n \cdot c_n,
\end{equation}
where $\gamma_n$ depends on the projection of the exposure vector $(e_i)$ onto the design matrix of the short model (explicit form in Appendix~\ref{app:gamma}). The bias is non-zero whenever $\mathrm{Cov}(d_j,\, c_n \cdot e_i) \neq 0$. This captures the confounding component; in the nonlinear MFRM, a specification bias from omitting $c_n \cdot e_i$ persists even at zero covariance (Section~\ref{sec:experiments}).
\end{observation}

The linear approximation in Observation~\ref{obs:ovb} ($r = 0.86$) serves solely as directional motivation for the bias structure; G-MFRM's Stage~3 inference (Eq.~\ref{eq:gmfrm}) uses the full nonlinear ordinal logistic likelihood and does not depend on this approximation. The bias combines a specification component from omitting $c_n \cdot e_i$, which persists regardless of confounding, with a confounding component from non-orthogonality between $d_j$ and $c_n \cdot e_i$. Monte Carlo simulation confirms that bias magnitude is approximately constant across confounding levels $\rho \in \{0, 0.3, 0.6, 0.9\}$, indicating that the omission of $c_n \cdot e_i$---not its correlation with $d_j$---is the primary driver (Figure~\ref{fig:bias_vs_rho}).

This confounding undermines both sequential estimation strategies. A bias-first approach, as in CyclicJudge \citep{cyclicjudge2026}, calibrates $d_j$ before estimating ability, but the resulting $\hat{\theta}$ is already inflated for contaminated models because the omitted $c_n \cdot e_i$ term has been absorbed into the ability estimates. A contamination-first approach, as in DCR \citep{dcr2025}, adjusts for contamination before addressing judge effects, but judge severity is then misattributed as contamination signal. Synthetic validation confirms this failure: with $N = 50$ models, $I = 100$ items, $J = 8$ judges, and 20 random seeds, the Pearson correlation between sequential estimation bias and true $c_n$ reaches $r = 0.86$ ($p < 0.001$). As illustrated in Figure~\ref{fig:ovb_bias}, the bias is not random but directional: contaminated models are systematically overestimated under both sequential orderings.

\section{Related Work}
\label{sec:related_work}

\paragraph{Judge Bias in LLM Evaluation.}
LLM-as-a-judge evaluations are subject to multiple systematic biases. \citet{wang2024positionbias} demonstrate that LLM judges exhibit strong position bias in pairwise evaluation, where swapping the order of candidate responses can flip the ranking; \citet{li2024splitmerge} propose a split-and-merge strategy to align position biases across evaluators. Beyond positional effects, \citet{koo2024cobbler} benchmark six cognitive biases in 15 LLM evaluators, finding significant bias in approximately 40\% of comparisons, and \citet{ye2024justice} quantify verbosity and self-enhancement biases. \citet{panickssery2024selffavor} show that LLMs systematically favor their own generations, a confound that affects any evaluation where a judge model shares a family with a target. On the correction side, CyclicJudge \citep{cyclicjudge2026} eliminates average bias via round-robin assignment but treats residual variation as genuine ability. Unsupervised Debiasing Alignment (UDA) \citep{uda2026} learns a neural K-factor reducing inter-judge deviation by 63.4\% without modeling contamination. \citet{fiedler2026bias_uncertainty} formalize judge variability via $J/\Delta J$ diagnostics. \citet{bavaresco2025judgebench} evaluate 11 LLMs as judges across 20 NLP tasks, finding large variance across datasets and emphasizing the need for task-specific validation. All of these methods model only the judge facet, absorbing contamination effects into ability estimates (Section~\ref{sec:confounding}).

\paragraph{Benchmark Contamination Detection and Avoidance.}
Benchmark contamination arises when evaluation items appear in training data, inflating performance estimates. Detection methods take multiple approaches: ConStat \citep{constat2024} uses performance-based statistical tests comparing original and rephrased items; Data Contamination Risk (DCR) \citep{dcr2025} quantifies contamination via a unified metric integrating multiple evidence sources; LogProber \citep{logprober2024} leverages token-level log-probabilities to disentangle memorization from genuine capability; and Min-K\% \citep{mink2024} detects pretraining data membership through outlier token probabilities. \citet{golchin2024timetravel} propose guided instruction prompting to detect instance-level contamination, while \citet{oren2024proving} provide provable statistical guarantees for black-box contamination detection. \citet{deng2024contamination} and \citet{sainz2023contamination} investigate the prevalence and measurement of contamination across modern benchmarks. On the judge side, \citet{preference_leakage2026} identify preference leakage, where judge-side familiarity with benchmark items inflates scores---a complementary phenomenon to G-MFRM's examinee-side focus. Contamination avoidance strategies include dynamic benchmarks such as LiveBench \citep{livebench2025}, private evaluation via TRUCE \citep{truce2024}, and efficient subset selection via tinyBenchmarks \citep{polo2024tinybenchmarks}. G-MFRM takes a complementary approach: rather than preventing contamination, it corrects for residual contamination in existing benchmarks while simultaneously modeling judge effects.

\paragraph{Item Response Theory (IRT) and MFRM for LLM Evaluation.}
