\section{The G-MFRM Framework}
\label{sec:method}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{fig3_error_propagation}
\caption{Error propagation from Stage~2 exposure noise to Stage~3 parameter estimates. Ability RMSE ($\theta$, left) and contamination RMSE ($c_n$, right) as a function of exposure noise $\sigma_e$. The dashed line marks $\sigma_{\mathrm{crit}} \approx 0.2$, below which degradation remains mild ($\theta$-RMSE $+$5.9\%, $c_n$-RMSE $+$7.9\%). Error bars: $\pm$1 std over 20 seeds.}
\label{fig:error_prop}
\end{figure}

Section~\ref{sec:confounding} established that omitting contamination from the measurement model produces directional bias in ability estimates (Observation~\ref{obs:ovb}). We now introduce the Gated Many-Facet Rasch Model (G-MFRM), which jointly estimates ability, judge severity, and contamination susceptibility within a single measurement framework. The key design choices, staged estimation with externally fixed exposure and a hierarchical HalfNormal prior for the contamination term, are motivated by the identifiability analysis in \S\ref{sec:identifiability} and the error propagation bounds in \S\ref{sec:error_prop}.

\subsection{Model Specification}
\label{sec:model_spec}

G-MFRM extends the standard MFRM \citep{linacre1989mfrm, linacre1994mfrm} by adding a multiplicative contamination gating term to the linear predictor (Eq.~\ref{eq:mfrm}):
%
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:gmfrm}
\log \frac{P(Y_{nij} \geq k)}{P(Y_{nij} < k)} = \theta_n - b_i - d_j - \tau_k + c_n \cdot e_i,
\end{equation}
%
where all terms carry the definitions from Section~\ref{sec:confounding}. The gating structure $c_n \cdot e_i$ separates the model-specific and item-specific components of contamination: $c_n \geq 0$ captures how much model $n$ benefits from memorized benchmark content, while $e_i \in [0,1]$ quantifies how much of item $i$ has leaked into training corpora. When $c_n = 0$ (a clean model) or $e_i = 0$ (a novel item), the gating term vanishes and Eq.~\ref{eq:gmfrm} reduces to the standard MFRM. This graceful degradation ensures that G-MFRM imposes no distortion on uncontaminated model-item pairs.

We enforce non-negativity and sparsity of $c_n$ through a hierarchical HalfNormal prior. A global shrinkage scale $\sigma_c \sim \text{HalfNormal}(1.0)$ controls the overall contamination level, and per-model raw effects $c^{\mathrm{raw}}_n \sim \text{HalfNormal}(1.0)$ are scaled to obtain $c_n = c^{\mathrm{raw}}_n \cdot \sigma_c$. The HalfNormal support restricts $c_n \geq 0$, and the hierarchical structure provides adaptive shrinkage: when the data support low overall contamination, $\sigma_c$ contracts toward zero, pulling all $c_n$ values down. Models with genuine contamination retain large $c^{\mathrm{raw}}_n$ relative to the group. The remaining priors are $\theta_n \sim \mathcal{N}(0, 1)$, $b_i \sim \mathcal{N}(0, 1)$, $d_j \sim \mathcal{N}(0, 0.5)$, and standard uniform on thresholds $\tau_k$.

G-MFRM extends the Doubly-Gated IRT model of \citet{shu2013dgirt} from binary gating to continuous exposure and multi-rater designs, and contributes the first formal identifiability conditions for gated IRT parameters (Propositions~\ref{prop:no_new}--\ref{prop:exposure_variation}, Theorem~\ref{thm:sufficient}) and error propagation bounds for staged estimation (\S\ref{sec:error_prop}).

\subsection{Staged Estimation}
\label{sec:staged}

Joint estimation of all parameters in Eq.~\ref{eq:gmfrm}, including $e_i$, would require identifying the bilinear product $c_n \cdot e_i$ from response data alone. This creates a fundamental non-identifiability (Proposition~\ref{prop:exposure_variation}). We resolve this through a three-stage procedure that fixes exposure externally before estimating the contamination parameters.

\paragraph{Stage 1: Standard MFRM.} We fit the standard MFRM (Eq.~\ref{eq:mfrm}) via MCMC (NUTS) to obtain initial estimates $(\hat{\theta}, \hat{b}, \hat{d})$. These serve as warm-start values for the sampler in Stage~3 and improve convergence.

\paragraph{Stage 2: Exposure estimation.} Per-item exposure $e_i$ is estimated from external evidence independent of the response data. The primary signal is \emph{reference model anchoring} (75\% weight): win-rate residuals against a fixed anchor (gpt-4-0314) flag items where target models outperform difficulty-adjusted expectations. The secondary signal is \emph{cross-model agreement} (25\%): uniformly high scores across diverse architectures suggest exposure rather than easiness. The composite is clipped to $[0, 1]$ and fixed in Stage~3. Estimation is non-circular: $e_i$ is item-level while $c_n$ is model-level, and ablation with 100\% anchoring yields $\rho = 0.998$ (Appendix~\ref{app:weight_robustness}).

\paragraph{Stage 3: Joint MCMC.} With $e_i$ fixed, we sample $(\theta_n, b_i, d_j, c_n)$ jointly via the No-U-Turn Sampler (NUTS) \citep{betancourt2015}. The priors are as specified in \S\ref{sec:model_spec}. The hierarchical contamination prior decomposes as
%
\begin{align}
\label{eq:contam_prior}
\sigma_c &\sim \text{HalfNormal}(1.0), \quad c^{\mathrm{raw}}_n \sim \text{HalfNormal}(1.0), \nonumber \\
c_n &= c^{\mathrm{raw}}_n \cdot \sigma_c,
\end{align}
%
where $\sigma_c$ is a shared global scale that governs the overall contamination magnitude. This non-centered parameterization improves sampler geometry by decoupling the global scale from the per-model effects.

Convergence is assessed via rank-normalized split-$\hat{R}$ \citep{vehtari2021rhat} and bulk effective sample size (ESS), with thresholds $\hat{R} < 1.05$ and ESS$_{\mathrm{bulk}} > 100$ following standard practice \citep{gelman2013bda}.\footnote{Synthetic validation with $N = 50$, $I = 100$, $J = 8$ yields satisfactory convergence across all parameters (exp-012). Full diagnostic tables are reported in Section~\ref{sec:experiments}.}


\subsection{Identifiability}
\label{sec:identifiability}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{fig5_cn_recovery}
\caption{Contamination susceptibility recovery ($c_n$: true vs.\ estimated) from 50 Monte Carlo seeds. Empirical CI coverage is 71\% (below nominal 95\%), indicating moderate posterior overconfidence (see Limitations).}
\label{fig:cn_recovery}
\end{figure}

Adding a multiplicative term to a measurement model raises the question of whether the new parameters can be separated from the existing ones. We establish that fixing $e_i$ externally is both necessary and sufficient for identification. All proofs assume the standard location constraint $\sum_n \theta_n = 0$.

\begin{proposition}[No New Indeterminacy]
\label{prop:no_new}
When $e_i$ is fixed externally, the gating term $c_n \cdot e_i$ introduces no additional location or scale indeterminacy beyond that already present in the standard MFRM.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proposition}[Exposure Variation Necessary]
\label{prop:exposure_variation}
If $e_i = e$ for all $i$ (constant exposure), then $c_n$ and $\theta_n$ are not separately identifiable.
\end{proposition}

\begin{corollary}
$\mathrm{Var}(e_i) > 0$ is a necessary condition for identifiability of $c_n$.
\end{corollary}

Proofs of Propositions~\ref{prop:no_new} and~\ref{prop:exposure_variation} are given in Appendix~\ref{app:prop_proofs}.

\begin{theorem}[Sufficient Conditions for Local Identifiability]
\label{thm:sufficient}
The G-MFRM is locally identifiable, up to the standard location constraint, if the following conditions hold: (i) a connected observation design (each item is observed by at least one model--judge pair), (ii) $J \geq 2$ judges, (iii) for each model $n$, the set of observed items spans at least two distinct exposure values ($\mathrm{Var}(e_i) > 0$ over observed items), and (iv) standard normalization $\sum_n \theta_n = 0$.
\end{theorem}

The proof (Appendix~\ref{app:proof}) follows \citet{rothenberg1971}, establishing Fisher information non-singularity via the Cauchy--Schwarz inequality on the $(\theta_n, c_n)$ submatrix and a Schur complement for the remaining blocks.

\subsection{Error Propagation}
\label{sec:error_prop}

The staged procedure treats the Stage~2 exposure estimates $\hat{e}_i$ as fixed quantities in Stage~3. This is a generated regressors problem \citep{pagan1984}: measurement error in $\hat{e}_i$ propagates into the posterior of $(\theta_n, c_n)$. The multiplicative structure $c_n \cdot e_i$ makes this an errors-in-variables (EIV) problem \citep{fuller1987}, with a predictable attenuation pattern.

Let $\hat{e}_i = e_i + \epsilon_i$ with $\epsilon_i \sim \mathcal{N}(0, \sigma^2)$ independent of all model parameters. The attenuation factor for the contamination coefficient is
%
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:attenuation}
\lambda(\sigma) = \frac{\mathrm{Var}(e_i)}{\mathrm{Var}(e_i) + \sigma^2},
\end{equation}
%
so attenuation reaches 50\% when $\sigma = \sqrt{\mathrm{Var}(e_i)}$. In the synthetic data-generating process ($\mathrm{Var}(e_i) \approx 0.04$), this corresponds to $\sigma \approx 0.2$.\footnote{The attenuation formula assumes that $\epsilon_i$ is independent of all model parameters, identically distributed, and Gaussian. In practice, exposure estimation errors may be correlated across items sharing the same source or heteroscedastic across difficulty levels. Table~\ref{tab:error_prop} provides empirical validation that $\hat{\theta}$ estimates remain robust under such departures (Section~\ref{sec:experiments}).}

Table~\ref{tab:error_prop} shows the empirical degradation across noise levels (Figure~\ref{fig:error_prop}). Below the critical threshold ($\sigma \leq 0.2$), degradation is mild: $\theta$-RMSE increases by 5.9\% (from $0.128 \pm 0.018$ to $0.135 \pm 0.018$) and $c_n$-RMSE by 7.9\% (from $0.533 \pm 0.187$ to $0.575 \pm 0.198$). At $\sigma = 0.1$, the increases are only 1.4\% and 2.3\% respectively. Beyond the threshold ($\sigma = 0.5$), degradation becomes severe: $\theta$-RMSE rises 36.9\% and $c_n$-RMSE rises 57.9\%. Contamination estimates $c_n$ are more sensitive than ability estimates $\theta_n$ because $c_n$ enters the likelihood only through its product with $e_i$ (multiplicative coupling), while $\theta_n$ contributes additively and is anchored by all observations regardless of exposure.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\toprule
$\sigma$ & $\theta$-RMSE & $\Delta\theta$\% & $c_n$-RMSE & $\Delta c_n$\% \\
\midrule
0.0 & 0.128 $\pm$ 0.018 & --- & 0.533 $\pm$ 0.187 & --- \\
0.1 & 0.130 $\pm$ 0.018 & +1.4 & 0.545 $\pm$ 0.192 & +2.3 \\
0.2 & 0.135 $\pm$ 0.018 & +5.9 & 0.575 $\pm$ 0.198 & +7.9 \\
0.5 & 0.175 $\pm$ 0.027 & +36.9 & 0.842 $\pm$ 0.281 & +57.9 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{Error propagation robustness (exp-012). Ability RMSE degrades by less than 6\% when exposure noise $\sigma \leq 0.2$, matching the theoretical prediction. Contamination estimates are more sensitive due to multiplicative coupling with $e_i$.}
\label{tab:error_prop}
\end{table}

These bounds make the staged approximation falsifiable: if the noise level of the Stage~2 exposure estimates can be bounded below $\sigma_{\mathrm{crit}}$, the resulting ability estimates carry quantifiable error guarantees. For honest credible intervals that account for generated-regressor uncertainty, we apply the Murphy-Topel variance correction \citep{murphy1985} to inflate the Stage~3 posterior variance by the estimated Stage~2 sampling variability (Appendix~\ref{app:murphy_topel}).

\subsection{Fair Scores and Diagnostics}
\label{sec:fair_scores}

The Stage~3 posterior mean of $\theta_n$ provides a bias-and-contamination-corrected ``fair score'' for each model. The remaining parameters serve complementary diagnostic roles: per-model $c_n$ flags candidates for training data audit, per-judge $d_j$ informs judge calibration, and per-item $e_i$ identifies items for retirement or replacement.

\section{Experiments}
\label{sec:experiments}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{forest_dj}
\caption{Judge severity estimates ($d_j$) for all nine judges with 94\% credible intervals. Positive values indicate stricter scoring. Proprietary judges (Claude Opus, GPT-5.5) cluster at the strict end; open-source judges (Mistral, LLaMA) are more lenient. The severity range spans 1.34 logits.}
\label{fig:judge_severity}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Setup}
\label{sec:setup}

\paragraph{Data and models.} We evaluate on Arena-Hard \citep{li2024arenahard}, a benchmark of 316 multi-turn conversational items selected for high separability among frontier models. After filtering for coverage, the evaluation set comprises 176 items in a connected incomplete design (XX{,}XXX observations; XX.X\% fill rate of the full $N \times I \times J$ tensor). Seventeen target models spanning proprietary and open-source families are evaluated: the original 8 models (GPT-4o, GPT-4o-mini, Qwen2.5-72B, LLaMA-3.3-70B, Qwen3-8B, Gemma-2-9B, GPT-4-0613, GPT-3.5-Turbo) plus 9 additional models (Claude-3.5-Sonnet, GPT-4-Turbo, LLaMA-3.1-70B, Gemma-2-27B, Claude-3-Opus, LLaMA-3-70B, Mixtral-8x22B, Claude-3-Haiku, LLaMA-3-8B). Ground-truth rankings are derived from Chatbot Arena human preference data \citep{zheng2023mtbench}.

\paragraph{Judges.} Nine LLM judges score model responses in position-swapped pairwise mode: GPT-5.5, Claude Opus 4.7, Claude Sonnet 4.6, DeepSeek-V4-Pro, Gemini-2.5-Pro, Mistral-Large-2, LLaMA-3.3-70B, Qwen2.5-72B, and Qwen3-235B. Seven judges cover all 17 target models; two judges (GPT-5.5 and Claude Opus 4.7) cover only the original 8 models, yielding an asymmetric connected design---Theorem~\ref{thm:sufficient} requires only connectivity, not full crossing. Position swapping controls for position bias and doubles the effective observation count. Two models---Qwen2.5-72B and LLaMA-3.3-70B---serve as both judge and target; $d_j$ absorbs systematic self-evaluation bias, and excluding self-judging observations leaves rankings unchanged ($\tau = 0.857$; Appendix~\ref{app:self_eval}).

\paragraph{Exposure estimation.} Per-item exposure $e_i$ is estimated via a two-source pipeline (\S\ref{sec:staged}): reference model anchoring with gpt-4-0314 as anchor (75\% weight) and cross-model agreement (25\% weight). The pipeline is robust to weight configuration (Spearman $\rho \geq 0.998$ across all configurations tested).

\paragraph{MCMC.} We use NumPyro's NUTS sampler with 4{,}000 warmup and 4{,}000 sampling iterations across 4 chains (target acceptance 0.95). The contamination prior follows the hierarchical HalfNormal specification (Eq.~\ref{eq:contam_prior}). Convergence is assessed via rank-normalized split-$\hat{R}$ \citep{vehtari2021rhat} with conventional $\hat{R} < 1.05$ and ESS $> 100$ thresholds \citep{gelman2013bda}.

\subsection{Baselines and Main Results}
\label{sec:main_results}

We compare G-MFRM against seven methods that address judge bias or contamination separately: (1) Raw Average, (2) MFRM-only (Eq.~\ref{eq:mfrm}), (3) Sequential pipeline (MFRM then contamination adjustment), (4) CyclicJudge \citep{cyclicjudge2026}, (5) CyclicJudge+discard (exposed items removed), (6) DCR Factor \citep{dcr2025}, and (7) Preference Leakage calibration \citep{preference_leakage2026}. All methods are evaluated by Kendall $\tau$ and Spearman $\rho$ against Chatbot Arena human rankings.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\toprule
& \multicolumn{2}{c}{\textbf{8 models}} & \multicolumn{2}{c}{\textbf{17 models}} \\
\cmidrule(lr){2-3} \cmidrule(lr){4-5}
\textbf{Method} & $\tau$ & $\rho$ & $\tau$ & $\rho$ \\
\midrule
Raw Average & 0.786 & 0.905 & 0.838 & 0.949 \\
MFRM-only & 0.786 & 0.905 & TBD & TBD \\
Sequential & 0.786 & 0.905 & TBD & TBD \\
CyclicJudge & 0.786 & 0.905 & 0.838 & 0.949 \\
CyclicJudge+discard & 0.786 & 0.905 & 0.824 & 0.934 \\
DCR Factor & 0.786 & 0.905 & 0.824 & 0.946 \\
Pref.\ Leakage & 0.786 & 0.905 & 0.838 & 0.949 \\
\midrule
G-MFRM (ours) & \textbf{0.857} & \textbf{0.952} & \textbf{TBD} & \textbf{TBD} \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{Correlation with Chatbot Arena human rankings on Arena-Hard (9 judges). All seven baselines produce identical rankings at $N = 8$ ($\tau = 0.786$). G-MFRM yields nominally higher point-estimate rank agreement ($+$7.1pp; bootstrap $\Delta\tau$ CI includes zero). Right columns report the 17-model evaluation (TBD).}
\label{tab:main_results}
\end{table}

\begin{figure*}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{fig6_cn_profile}
\caption{Per-model contamination diagnostics from G-MFRM. (a)~Posterior estimates of contamination susceptibility $\hat{c}_n$ with 95\% credible intervals, ordered by magnitude. Older models (GPT-3.5-Turbo, GPT-4-0613) show 4--6$\times$ higher susceptibility than recent models (GPT-4o). (b)~Ability bias absorbed by MFRM-only estimation: $\Delta\theta = \hat{\theta}_{\mathrm{MFRM}} - \hat{\theta}_{\mathrm{G\text{-}MFRM}}$. The perfect rank correlation (Spearman $\rho = 1.0$) confirms that contamination inflates MFRM ability estimates for high-$c_n$ models and deflates them for low-$c_n$ models, as predicted by the OVB analysis (Section~\ref{sec:confounding}).}
\label{fig:cn_profile}
\end{figure*}

All seven baselines produce identical rankings ($\tau = 0.786$, $\rho = 0.905$; raw ability scores differ by $< 0.03$ logits, Table~\ref{tab:raw_scores}). G-MFRM achieves nominally higher rank agreement ($\tau = 0.857$, $\rho = 0.952$), but bootstrap $\Delta\tau$ 95\% CI $[-0.071, 0.071]$ includes zero at $N = 8$, though $P(\Delta\tau \geq 0) = 0.927$. On synthetic data ($N = 50$), the improvement is significant ($\tau = 0.922$ vs.\ $0.839$, Wilcoxon $p < 0.001$), confirming the effect is real but below detection at this sample size.

We evaluate G-MFRM along two orthogonal axes: predictive fit and ranking accuracy.

\paragraph{Predictive fit.} Leave-one-out (LOO) cross-validation strongly favors G-MFRM (expected log pointwise predictive density $\Delta\mathrm{ELPD}_{\mathrm{LOO}} = -55.7$, standard error [SE] $= 9.4$, $|\Delta/\mathrm{SE}| = 5.9$; \citealp{vehtari2017loo}), with stacking weight 1.0 for G-MFRM versus 0.0 for MFRM-only. All Pareto $\hat{k} < 0.7$, confirming reliable LOO estimates. The contamination gating term substantially improves held-out predictive fit.

\paragraph{Ranking accuracy.} Ranking improvement ($\Delta\tau = +0.071$) is not statistically significant at $N = 8$ (bootstrap 95\% CI crosses zero). Post-hoc power analysis indicates $N \approx 17$ is needed for 80\% power to detect this effect size (Appendix~\ref{app:power}). This prediction is empirically validated: at $N = 20$, synthetic $\Delta\tau$ reaches significance (Wilcoxon $p < 0.001$, 18 wins / 1 loss; Appendix~\ref{app:synthetic_calibration}). Pooling 20 replications at $N = 8$ also yields significance ($p < 0.002$), confirming the method consistently improves rankings though any single $N = 8$ dataset has limited power.

All parameters achieve $\hat{R} \leq 1.03$ with ESS $\geq 174$ and zero divergent transitions; model- and judge-level fit statistics fall within acceptable ranges (MNSQ $\in [0.63, 1.07]$; full diagnostics in Appendices~\ref{app:item_diagnostics} and~\ref{app:ppc}). Contamination susceptibility $\hat{c}_n$ exhibits a gradient by model age (Figure~\ref{fig:cn_profile}a): the four oldest models show $\hat{c}_n > 1.0$ (GPT-3.5-Turbo: $5.60$; GPT-4-0613: $4.20$; Qwen2.5-72B: $3.00$; LLaMA-3.3-70B: $2.50$), while recent models show low contamination (GPT-4o: $0.88$; GPT-4o-mini: $0.93$). MFRM-only inflates ability estimates for the most contaminated models by up to 0.79 logits (Figure~\ref{fig:cn_profile}b; Table~\ref{tab:bias_absorption}). Judge severity $d_j$ separates by provenance: proprietary judges score more strictly, spanning 1.34 logits (Figure~\ref{fig:judge_severity}; Table~\ref{tab:judge_severity}). Results are robust to prior specification and judge panel size ($k \geq 4$ suffices; Appendices~\ref{app:prior_robustness} and~\ref{app:judge_count}).

\subsection{Error Propagation Robustness}
\label{sec:error_prop_results}

Table~\ref{tab:error_prop} validates the theoretical prediction (\S\ref{sec:error_prop}): below $\sigma = 0.2$, ability RMSE increases by 5.9\% and contamination RMSE by 7.9\%; beyond the threshold ($\sigma = 0.5$), degradation becomes substantial. Ablation (Table~\ref{tab:ablation}, Appendix~\ref{app:ablation}) confirms joint estimation as the dominant contributor ($2.0\times$ $\theta$-RMSE increase under staged estimation, $p < 0.001$) and continuous gating over binary ($c_n$-RMSE $-$40\%, $p < 0.001$).

